End result is end result.

Even if AI discovered these solutions today, it would take about 7-10 years to produce sufficient evidence they solve anything in humans. It’s going to speed some things up, and there’s really good work being done by people to try and facilitate that - but the amount of data we’d need to generate to reduce the timeline to a few years…I’m not going to say anything is impossible but we’d need to pretty much redirect every dollar of investing on AI infrastructure towards bio. If the pace of discovery accelerates beyond what we are used to (especially in diseases and targets where causal biology is well understood), I won’t be surprised at all. Like AI creating better immunotherapies I’d say is likely. AI curing cancer by 2030 would not just be surprising it would mean something massive (entirely outside of AI) had changed.

@TexasOncologist @tszzl Great point and relevant to the AI aspect which is still ultimately going to be driven by where resources are spent.

We would need something like 3 orders of magnitude more data in order to move from AI being good at large molecule discovery & design to AI being able to approximate toxicology. Again, not impossible but that data needs to be generated in the real world - by physical labs, in cells that work at the speed of biology regardless of how good the algorithm is or how much compute we have.

